          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Baas"} Nils Baas
            has been emphasizing for many years, in print and in private communication, the conviction that the usual notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} infinity-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-category"} omega-category
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            are
            %strong not
            naturally suited for describing
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordisms"} extended cobordisms
                such as appearing in
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangle+hypothesis"} tangle hypothesis
                %li
                  %p
                    in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended quantum field theory
            %li
              %p hierarchical systems such as appearing in
              %ul
                %li
                  %p complex systems;
                %li
                  %p biology.
          %p
            The point is essentially that the
            %em directedness
            of morphisms and — related to that — the binary notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            in categories and higher categories are notions alien to these contexts, which in applications have to and are essentially removed again in a second step by adding extra structure and requiring further properties, such as various monoidal structures and dualities, which allow to change the direction of morphisms, to collect objects together, etc.
          %p
            In contrast to that, Baas pointed out that more naturally the above situations are thought of from the beginning in terms of hierarchies of what he calls
            = succeed "," do
              %em bonds
            = succeed "." do
              %em bound by the bond
          %p A sketch of a generic such a situation of hierarchical bonds is a diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 4
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi b
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi b
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi b
                            %mn 3
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 3
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi β
                            %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\beta_2 &amp;&amp;=&amp;&amp; \beta_2 &amp;&amp; \beta_4
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  b_1 &amp;\to&amp; B &amp;\leftarrow&amp; b_2
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow  &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  \\beta_1 &amp;&amp; b_3 &amp;&amp; \beta_3
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \beta_5
                  }
          %p
            where a bond
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            binds sub-bonds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_1, b_2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_3
            which in turn bind sub-bonds
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_i
          %p
            For instance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            might be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordism"} extended cobordism
            with three boundary components
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_1, b_2, b_3
            which in turn have pieces of boundary components
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_i
          %p
            Nils Baas has made, in print and in private communication, suggestions for a formalization of such systems of hierarchical bonds and coined the term
            %strong hyperstructures
            for these. Some references are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On Higher Order Structures
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00403"} arXiv:1509.00403
                (2015), 15 pp.
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher order architecture of collections of objects
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0344"} arXiv:1409.0344
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas, N.C. Seeman and A. Stacey,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Synthesising topological links
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas, D.V. Fedorov, A.S. Jensen, K. Riisager, A.G. Volosniev and N.T. Zinner,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher-Order Brunnian Structures and Possible Physical Realizations
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0746"} arXiv:1205.0746
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em New states of matter suggested by new topological structures
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1012.2698"} arXiv:1012.2698
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On structure and organization: an organizing principle
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6228"} arXiv:1201.6228
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas and N.C. Seeman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the chemical synthesis of new topological structures
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em New structures in complex systems
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hyperstructures, Topology and Datasets
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hyperstructures as Abstract Matter
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas, A. Ehresmann and J.-P. Vanbremeersch,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hyperstructures and Memory Evolutive Systems
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Higher order cognitive structures and processes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Toward a Science of Consciousness
            %li
              %p
                Nils A. Baas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hyper-structures as a tool in nanotechnology
          %h1#remarks Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The notion of replacing morphisms by
                %em bonds
                is familiar and concretely realized at least in a hierarchy of depth 2 in the context of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spans"} spans
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospans"} cospans
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                produces diagrams like the above sketch of a generic bond system.
            %li
              %p
                Accordingly, the notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispan"} multispan
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-cospan"} multi-cospan
                may come close to exhibiting some crucial aspects of the idea that motivated the concept of hyperstructures.
          %h1#blog_discussion Blog discussion
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                David Corfield,
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/11/category_theory_and_biology.html"} Category Theory and Biology
              %ul
                %li
                  The birth of the idea of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispans"} multispans
                  as a formalization of hyperstructures is mentioned in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/11/category_theory_and_biology.html#c013165"} this comment
          %p
            Its application to the description of extended cobordisms and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangle+hypothesis"} generalized tangle hypothesis
            is topic of some thought chatted about at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/05/hopkinslurie_on_baezdolan.html#c021486"} this entry
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike
              \: Shouldn’t we allow “oriented bonds” as well? I am thinking of the case of, say, rings and modules, where a module
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              that has right actions by rings
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and a left action by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              should have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R,S,T
              as “sub-bonds” but with different “orientations.” And the “gluing” operation is tensor product, which only works if one module is a left module and the other is a right module. This example also goes up in dimension, for instance modules over algebras over rings. However, it seems that one would also needs this for cobordisms; aren’t you only allowed to glue cobordisms along boundaries whose orientations match?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs
              \: right, the module example currently does not really fit yet. The reason seems to be that the would-be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger
              functor is not the identity on objects here, but sends objects to their opposites.
            %p Concerning the cobordisms: in the cospan picture, every boundary component explicitly comes with its embedding into the cobordism. If two boundary components are equal as unembedded objects, then the pushout will automatically glue the corresponding cobordisms in the right way by identifying the components as embedded pieces. Do you see what I mean. Maybe I am mixed up about this.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike
              \: Yes, the point is that a lot of these examples aren’t really dagger at all, only
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed
              (which I would prefer to call “autonomous”). My point about cobordisms has to do with orientations. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              are two manifolds sharing a boundary component
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %em oriented
              then the gluing will only inherit an orientation if
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              and
              = succeed "\u2019s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is not the same as an oriented cobordism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs
              \: True. I am not sure about the best answer yet, but have thought about the following:
            %p Suppose we have a span
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ←
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \leftarrow C \to B
            %p
              and want to think of its index objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              to carry an orientation which gets reversed when we flip the legs around to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ←
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B \leftarrow C \to A
                    \\,.
            %p
              We might be able to emulate this within the setting described below by supposing that there are two different dummy objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
              around which behave at least to some extent like initial objects and that the original span was secretly really the multispan:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mo ←
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↖
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∘
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \bullet
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    A &amp;\leftarrow&amp; C &amp;\to&amp; B
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \nwarrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\circ
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p Now, if in this multispan we want to exchange the original two legs, we could for instance rotate the entire multispan by a half turn to get
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo ←
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↖
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \circ
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    B &amp;\leftarrow&amp; C &amp;\to&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \nwarrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\bullet
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p This would introduce the desired orientation on objects, as the second multispan behaves like:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo ←
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B^{op} \leftarrow C \to A^{op}
                    \\,.
            %p For this to work out as desired, we would of course have to concentrate on planar multispans and disallow identification of multispans that can be turned into each other by reflections at lines in the plane.
            %p
              %em> Mike
              \: It seems as though restricting to planar multispans would be quite a serious thing to do.
          %hr/
          %h1#laboratory_section Laboratory section
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs
              \: I am thinking about a way to formalize the idea of hyperstructure with a concrete application to extended quantum field theory and the Baez-Dolan hypothesis in mind. I would like to develop this in the following here on this page. If things work out as hoped for, this should eventually become an entry in its own right. If not, this should eventually be discarded.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie
              In this connection I would like to draw attention here to
            %ul
              %li
                D.W.Jones, Polyhedral
                = succeed "-complexes," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "-complexes," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %p The idea here was to give expression to the notion of: what is wrong with pentagons? Or rhombic dodecahedra? as part of the basic models. Two major problems were solved:
            %ol
              %li
                %p What should be the basic cells, and the polyhedral category?
              %li
                %p How should one orient or more the basic cells?
            %p The answer to the first question is given in terms of cells with polyhedral boundary and in which there is a shellability condition. The answer to the second question is in terms of each cell has a marked face. This turns out to imply orientation, but is more of a homotopy condition than a homology condition, i.e. is stronger than the latter. There a relation to certain posets studied by Bjorner.
            %p On the other hand this theory is probably more group than category oriented, there is a tendency to imply inverses.
            %p
              The algebra comes in by defining
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  poly-
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                  \-complexes
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+element"} thin element
          %h2#idea_2 Idea
          %p
            %strong Motivation
          %p
            A
            %em bond system
            or
            %em hyperstructure
            as intended in the following should be
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a conglomerate of
                %em cells
                each of which is equipped with a prescribed collection of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em sub-cells
            %li
              %p such that whenever two such cell complexes coincide on any one part of their sub-cell complex, there exists a cell which can be regarded as gluing the two cells along this sub-cell complex.
          %p
            The idea is hence akin to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric definition of higher category
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                there is no fixed choice of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} geometric shapes for higher structures
                (such as
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe"} globe
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplices
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube"} cube
                %em all
                possible sub-cell complexes are admitted;
            %li
              %p
                there is no notion of directionality imposed on the cell complexes, and in particular
                %em no ordering
                on the sub-cells of a given cell, neither binary into source and target cells as in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe+category"} globular
                higher categories, nor linearly into indexed faces as in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplicial
                higher categories.
          %p
            The idea is that a hyperstructure is like a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} infinity-category
            but capturing this notion more directly than an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-dagger-operation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
          %p
            %strong Formalization
          %p
            To formalize this, the idea is to observe that every
            %em cell
            with its complex of sub-cells should naturally form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            under inclusions of sub-cells. Since for any collection of sub-cells which share a common collection of sub-sub-cells the result of gluing these sub-cells along their common sub-sub-cells should again be a sub-cell, this poset should be closed under
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
          %p
            Therefore a
            %em bond system
            or
            %em hyperstructure
            should in particular assign to every poset with colimits a collection to be interpreted as the collection of cells whose sub-cell structure is of the form given by the poset. Such an assignment would naturally allow to restrict any cell to any of its sub-cells along an inclusion of posets to obtain another cell, and to extend any cell to a cell with richer but degenerate sub-cell structure along a surjection of posets. This clearly suggests that the
            %em bond system
            or
            %em hyperstructure
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
          %p
            Given this, the
            %strong gluing condition
            or
            %strong sewing condition
            that for any collection of cells which match on a part of their sub-cell complex there exists a cell obtained by gluing the cells along their common sub-cells can be formulated in a way analogous to the horn-filling condition in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            by requiring that the obvious map from the collection of cells which have the right shape of being glued cells to the collection of cells that admit gluing is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
          %p
            %strong Examples
          %p
            The archetypical example of a
            %em bond system
            or
            %em hyperstructure
            as intended in the following is supposed to be the collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MultiCoSpans
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MultiCoSpans(C)
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispan"} multi co-span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with colimits. Since a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispan"} multi co-span
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is supposed to be nothing but the image of a poset in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em bond structure
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multispan"} multi co-span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Posets
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Posets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em gluing condition
            in that for all multi-cospans which coincide in parts there exists in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the colimit over their joint diagram. For ordinary
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
          %p
            %strong The hyperstructure of hyperstructures
          %p
            As there is a canonical notion of homo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
            s of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            we naturally obtain a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %em bond systems
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em hyperstructures
          %p
            This allows then finally to formalize one of the situations motivating the notion of hyperstructure in the first place, that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended quantum field theory
            %em topological
            QFT this is expected to be a morphism (or more general span) of hyperstructures
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ExtendedCobordisms
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %mi MultiCoSpans
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Top
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi MultiSpans
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mo :
                          %mn ∞
                          %msub
                            %mi Vect
                            %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  ExtendedCobordisms :=
                  MultiCoSpans(Top) &amp;\to&amp; MultiSpans(S)
                  \=:
                  \\infty Vect_S
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where multi-cospans in the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            are thought of as modelling
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordism"} extended cobordism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cospans+in+Algebraic+Topology"} Cospans in Algebraic Topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (multi co-spans in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi op
